{"records":[{"id":"dby7kdtzk8","title":"Cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-Positive Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma, not otherwise specified: A systematic review and comparative analysis with Epstein-Barr Virus-negative, leg type","description":"Supplement 1. PRISMA flow diagram of literature search and study selection. \nEBV+ DLBCL, NOS, Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified; PRISMA, Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses.\n\nSupplement 2. Histopathological features of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified. (A) Pleomorphic atypical large cells in a background rich in reactive cells such as lymphohistiocytes and plasma cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). (B) Monotonous sheets of immunoblast-like large cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). Atypical large lymphoid cells of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified highlighted by (C) MUM-1 (x200), (D) BCL-2 (x200), and (E) CD30 (x200); Epstein-Barr Virus infection detected by (F) positive Epstein-Barr encoding region in situ hybridization (x200).\n\nSupplement 3. Survival analysis of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified (EBV+ DLBCL, NOS). (A) Difference in overall survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and primary cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-negative diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, leg type (EBV- DLBCL, leg type). (B) Comparison in survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and concurrent cutaneous and extracutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-27T07:21:33.083Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dby7kdtzk8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dby7kdtzk8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Woo Jin ","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma","Lymphoma of the Skin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tfg83w73v3","title":"Epstein-Barr Virus Induced One-Carbon Metabolism Drives B Cell Transformation","description":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) causes Burkitt, Hodgkin, and post-transplant B-cell lymphomas. How EBV remodels metabolic pathways to support rapid B-cell outgrowth remains largely unknown. To gain insights, primary human B-cells were profiled by tandem-mass-tag based proteomics at rest and at 9 time points after infection. >8000 host and 29 viral proteins were quantified, revealing mitochondrial remodeling and induction of one-carbon (1C) metabolism. EBV-encoded EBNA2 and its target MYC were required for upregulation of the central mitochondrial 1C enzyme MTHFD2, which played key roles in EBV-driven B-cell growth and survival. MTHFD2 was critical for maintaining elevated NADPH levels in infected cells, and oxidation of mitochondrial NADPH diminished B-cell proliferation. Tracing studies underscored contributions of 1C to nucleotide synthesis, NADPH production and redox defense. EBV upregulated import and synthesis of serine to augment 1C flux. Our results highlight EBV-induced 1C as a potential therapeutic target and provide a new paradigm for viral onco-metabolism.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-06-28T09:48:57.435Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tfg83w73v3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tfg83w73v3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Weekes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolism","Epstein-Barr Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y395v54y8k","title":"GPCR signaling contributes to immune characteristics of microenvironment and process of EBV-induced lymphomagenesis","description":"Natural killer T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous lymphoid neoplasm of natural killer or cytotoxic T cells in association with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. In the present study, we performed a comprehensive proteogenomic characterization of 112 NKTCL, incorporating genomics, transcriptomics, and deep-scale proteomics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-22T07:19:27.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y395v54y8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y395v54y8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dvsrtknwh5","title":"Epstein–Barr Virus Immunoevasins, Associated Processes and Protein-Protein Interactions ","description":"This dataset contains the supplementary materials for manuscript entitled: A Database-Driven In Silico Approach to Identify Epstein–Barr Virus Immunoevasins, Associated Processes and Protein-Protein Interactions ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-02T15:30:59.401Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dvsrtknwh5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dvsrtknwh5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Norazlin","last_name":"Abdul Aziz","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Host-Pathogen Interaction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7gwtb7mgrr","title":"Genomic and Transcriptomic Characterization of NKTCL","description":"Natural-killer/T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous entity of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. To identify molecular subtypes of NKTCL based on genomic structural alterations and EBV sequences, we performed multi-omics study on 128 biopsy samples of newly diagnosed NKTCL and defined three prominent subtypes.\n\n ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-02T03:32:10.22Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7gwtb7mgrr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7gwtb7mgrr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genome Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5v545cw8t7","title":"Epstein-Barr virus BNRF1 destabilizes SMC5/6 cohesin complexes to evade its restriction of replication compartments. Yiu et al.","description":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) persistently infects most people worldwide. Delivery of ~170 kilobase EBV genomes to nuclei, and use of nuclear membrane-less replication compartments (RC) for their lytic cycle amplification, necessitate evasion of intrinsic antiviral responses. Proteomic analysis identified that upon B-cell infection or lytic reactivation, EBV depletes the chromosome maintenance cohesin SMC5/6, which has major chromosome maintenance roles and DNA damage repair. The major tegument protein BNRF1 targeted SMC5/6 complexes by a ubiquitin proteasome pathway dependent on calpain proteolysis and cullin-7. In the absence of BNRF1, SMC5/6 associated with R-loop structures, including at the viral lytic origin of replication, and interfered with RC formation and encapsidation. CRISPR analysis highlighted RC restriction roles of SMC5/6 components involved in DNA entrapment and SUMOylation. Our studies highlight SMC5/6 as a key intrinsic immune sensor and restriction factor for a human herpesvirus RC and have implications for the pathogenesis of EBV-associated cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-03T06:55:14.952Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5v545cw8t7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5v545cw8t7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Stephanie Pei Tung","last_name":"Yiu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pbsb83hb2p","title":"Integrating Cell-Free Epstein-Barr Virus DNA with the 9th Edition TNM Staging for Prognostic Stratification for Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma","description":"All study-level data, including training cohort, internal validation cohorts for M0 NPC patients as well as post-IC cohorts are deposited in these datasets. Any additional information required to reanalyze the data reported in this paper is available after approval by the corresponding author and Sun Yat-sen University Cancer Center.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-13T15:39:27.072Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pbsb83hb2p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pbsb83hb2p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jun","last_name":"Ma","orcid":"0000-0002-1137-9349"},{"first_name":"Xiao-Jing","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gao-Yuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Guo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kyrie","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei-Wei","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Clinical Data Collection"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8ghvvk587j","title":"Integrating Cell-Free Epstein-Barr Virus DNA with the 9th Edition TNM Staging for Prognostic Stratification for Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma","description":"All study-level data, including training cohort, internal and external validation cohorts for M0 NPC patients are deposited in this dataset. Any additional information required to reanalyze the data reported in this paper is available after approval by the corresponding author and Sun Yat-sen University Cancer Center.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-12T01:29:51.951Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8ghvvk587j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8ghvvk587j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shuh","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gaoyuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiao-Jing","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei-Wei","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Guo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jun","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Clinical Data Collection"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dzdnwnxyrv","title":"Gene body methylation in Myxozoa","description":"Oxford-Nanopore MinION sequencing, is a PCR-free method that retains epigenetic markers and provides direct quantitative information about DNA methylation. Using this long-read sequencing technology, we present complete and near-complete assemblies of five myxozoan genomes free from host DNA contamination. These genomes did not match any existing sequences in public databases, suggesting a need for updated sequence data for these elusive parasites. The quality of the assemblies surpassed that of existing assemblies, and methylation analysis indicated the presence of DNA methylation in the GC-rich regions within gene bodies. Enzymes related to DNA methylation were also identified in the genomes. Experimental and in silico analyses revealed variable methylation levels across genomes, suggesting a \"transcription switch\" mechanism regulating gene expression. Overall, this study highlights the importance of long-read sequencing methods to better understand epigenetic processes in parasites like myxozoans. The findings challenge existing paradigms and could inform new strategies to control myxozoan fish pathogens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-20T23:40:26.048Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dzdnwnxyrv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dzdnwnxyrv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ANTONIO","last_name":"STARCEVIC","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rayline","last_name":"Figueredo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juliana","last_name":"Naldoni","orcid":"0000-0002-6764-448X"},{"first_name":"Lincoln","last_name":"Corrêa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Beth","last_name":"Okamura","orcid":"0000-0001-7279-715X"},{"first_name":"Edson","last_name":"Adriano","orcid":"0000-0002-6903-9531"},{"first_name":"Paul","last_name":"Long","orcid":"0000-0001-6698-4602"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Epigenetics","Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s5d8fkzwhj","title":"A Study of Viral-Mediated AURKB Cleavage Promotes Cell  Segregation and Tumorigenesis. Zhu Q et al","description":"Aurora B (AURKB) kinase, a central regulator of chromosome segregation and cytokinesis, is aberrantly expressed in various cancer cells. However, the relationship of AURKB and oncogenic viruses in cancer progression remains unclear. Here, we reveal that N-cleaved isoforms of AURKB exist in several oncovirus-associated tumor cells and patient cancer tissues, including Kaposi’s sarcoma herpesvirus (KSHV), Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), and human papillomavirus virus (HPV). Mechanistically, in KSHV-infected tumor cells，the latent viral antigen LANA cleaves AURKB Asp76 in a serine protease-dependent manner. The N’-cleaved AURKB relocalizes to the spindle pole and promotes the metaphase-to-telophase transition in mitotic cells. Introduction of N’-AURKB but not C’-AURKB promotes colony formation and malignant growth of tumor cells in vitro and in vivo using a murine xenograft model. Altogether, our findings uncover a proteolytic cleavage mechanism by which oncoviruses induce cancer cell segregation and tumorigenesis.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-03-25T15:02:35.698Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s5d8fkzwhj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s5d8fkzwhj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qing","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling","last_name":"Ding","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenguo","last_name":"Zi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shujun","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chong","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuyan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caixia","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenghong","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fang","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiliang","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncovirus"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121205,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":150,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121205,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":120652,"restricted":63,"embargoed":490},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2097},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":649},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":384},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":378},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":303},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":241},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":208}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":118843},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1674},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":177},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":123},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":72},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":50},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":455},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":403},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":350},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":312},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":270}]}}